:Title: Will's Guide to Tintin Macros
:Author: Will Guaraldi



OVERVIEW
========

This document started out as a specification for how Tintin handles
commands and variable expansion so that I could then emulate it in
the Tintin evalmode in Lyntin.  However, outside of that original mission, 
it may also be a good guide to Tintin behavior and help in writing 
Tintin macros.

User input is handled in this order:

1. User input is checked to see if it's a command.  If it's a command
   then it gets handled by the function that handles that command
   per the behavior described above.  See `command handling`_.

2. User input is passed through the alias filter.  If any
   aliases are expanded, we recurse over the input and start the
   process again.  If there are no aliases to expand, we move on
   to the next step.  See `alias filter`_.

3. User input is passed through the speedwalk filter.  If the
   user input is expanded into speedwalk dirs, these dirs are
   then recursed over.  See `speedwalk filter`_.

4. User input is passed through the variable filter.  Here we
   expand any variables and then recurse over the input and start
   the process again.  If there are no variables to expand, we
   move on to the next step.  See `variable filter`_.

Each of these steps is detailed further.


.. _`command handling`:

COMMAND INPUT
=============

Lyntin handles commands before handling user input destined to the mud.
The following details the behavior of the various Tintin commands.


ACTION COMMAND
--------------

syntax: #action {trigger} {action}

The action_cmd doesn't evaluate anything when the command is processed.
The trigger is evaluated every time we're checking mud text to see if
it triggered an action.  If the mud text did trigger an action, then
we evaluate the action predicate by passing it through handleUserInput
just like regular user input would be handled.

::

   > #action {test} {#showme test}

creates "test" -> "#showme test".

::

   > #var {a} {a1}
   > #var {b} {b1}
   > #action {$a} {#showme $b}

creates "$a" -> "#showme $b" where "$a" gets expanded every time we
check for that action trigger and $b gets expanded by the showme_cmd.


ALIAS COMMAND
-------------

syntax: #alias {name} {alias}

Aliases get a little interesting because they add the placement variable
issue.  The alias name is evaluated at the time the alias_cmd is being
evaluated--prior to actually adding the alias to our list of aliases.
The alias predicate is evaluated when the alias is expanded by user
input.

::

   > #alias {test} {test2}

creates "test" -> "test2".

::

   > #var {a} {a1}
   > #var {b} {b1}
   > #alias {test} {$a}

creates "test" -> "$a" where $a will be expanded later when the user
kicks off the alias by typing "test ...".

::

   > #var {a} {a1}
   > #var {b} {b1}
   > #alias {$a} {$b}

creates "a1" -> "$b" where $b will be expanded later when the user
kicks off the alias by typing "a1 ...".


GAG COMMAND
-----------

#gag {text}

#gag is actually just a shorthand for #sub {} {text}.  If the gagged
text is found in an incoming line, the line is removed from the mud
output and never displayed to the user.

::

   > #gag text
   > #gag this is a gag
   > #gag {oh my gagness}

will gag all incoming lines that have "text", "this is a gag", and 
"oh my gagness" in them.

::

   > #var {a} {b}
   > #gag {$a}

will now gag all incoming lines that have $a in them--where $a
is expanded at the time of checking for gags.


HIGHLIGHT COMMAND
-----------------

#highlight {style} {text}

::

   > #high {blue} {some text}
   > #high {b red} {oh my highlight}

will highlight "some text" with a blue foreground and "oh my highlight"
with a red background.


IF COMMAND
----------

#if {expr} {action} {elseaction}

The if_cmd is pretty straight-forward.  It should be noted that Tintin
cannot handle string matching.  So #if {"a" == "a"} will get all unhappy.

Lyntin does some minor changes to the expression, but then passes it
to Python to be evaluated.  So long as it comes back as something that
we can infer true/false from, we can handle it.

::

   > #if {1=1} {#showme hello}

will print "hello"

::

   > #var a 1
   > #if {$a == 1} {#showme hello}

will print "hello"

::

   > #var a 1
   > #if {$a = 1} {$a}

will run "1" through the command input to be evaluated.


MATH COMMAND
------------

#math {var} {expr}

::

   > #math {myvar} {1+1}
   > #math myvar2 {$myvar + 5}
   > #math {myvar3} {$myvar * $myvar2}

will create three variables.  myvar = 2, myvar2 = 7, myvar3 = 14.


NOP COMMAND
-----------

#nop whatever

It's a no operation.  Anything after #nop is ignored up to the next ;.


SHOWME COMMAND
--------------

#showme {text...}

::

   > #showme text

will print "text".

::

   > #var {a} {a1}
   > #showme $a

will print "a1".

::

   > #var {a} {a1}
   > #action {$a} {test}
   > #showme $a

will print "a1" then will trigger the action which will do "test".


SUBSTITUTE COMMAND
------------------

#substitute {item} {substitute}

::

   > #sub {test} {TEST}

creates "test" -> "TEST".

::

   > #var {a} {b}
   > #sub {$a} {TEST}
   > #sub {oh my sub} {$a}

$a in both cases is expanded at the time we're checking for substitutes in
the incoming mud text.

::

   > #sub {hooplah} {.}
   > #sub {hooplah2} {}

Lines with hooplah are now removed and all instances of hooplah2 are
removed (not the line--just the word hooplah2).

In Lyntin, the following will work:

::

   > #sub {hooplah} {\.}

The . is escaped, so all instances of "hooplah" will be replaced with . .


VARIABLE COMMAND
----------------

#var {name} {value}

::

   > #var {a} {b}

creates "$a" which expands to "b".

::

   > #var {c} {d}
   > #var {$a} {test}

creates "$b" which expands to "test"--the variables in name are
expanded before creating the new variable.


.. _`alias filter`:

ALIAS FILTER
============

The alias filter looks at the first token and checks to see if it
expands into an alias.  Placement variables are used by aliases to 
let you to specify arguments to get expanded into the alias 
predicate.

::

   > #ali {hsay} {house chat $0}
   > hsay this is cool

will result in "house chat this is cool" being sent to the mud.

::

   > #ali {gc} {give %1 to %2}
   > gc pipe joe

will result in "give pipe to joe" being sent to the mud.


Placement variables get expanded depending on their nesting depth
at the time the alias is expanded.

::

   > #ali {test} {#ali {tst2} {%1}}

creates "test" -> "#ali {tst2} {%1}".

::

   > test hello

creates "tst2" -> "%1".

::

   > #ali {test} {#ali {tst2} {%%1}}

creates "test" -> "#ali {tst2} {%%1}".

::

   > test hello

creates "tst2" -> "hello".


In the first example, the %1 is in two nestings of brackets.  But it
only has one %.  Thus it does not get expanded to "hello" and the
alias "tst2" -> "%1".

In the second example, the %%1 is in two nestings of brackets and
has two % signs.  Thus it gets expanded to "hello" and the alias 
"tst2" -> "hello".

The number of % signs indicates the nesting depth at which the placement
variable will be expanded.  If the nesting depth equals the number
of % signs, then the variable will be expanded, otherwise it's left as
is.

Placement variables in the predicate can start with a % or a $.  The
rules still apply to $.

examples::

   > #alias {test} {say %1 %2 %3}

is the same as::

   > #alias {test} {say $1 $2 $3}


The way Tintin distinguishes between placement variables and regular 
variables is by the name.  If the variable name is all digits, then 
it's a placement variable.  Otherwise it's a regular variable.

Lyntin adds to this a bit and allows Python-like splice syntax for 
placement variables::

   > #alias {test} {say $1:5}
   > test 1 2 3 4 5

will result in "say 1 2 3 4" being sent to the mud.

::

   > #alias {test} {say $1:3 to $4}
   > test 1 2 3 4 5

will result in "say 1 2 3 to 4" being sent to to the mud.


